Let $\vec{u}$, $\vec{v}$, $\vec{w}$ be vectors and $c$, $d$ be scalars.

Addition Properties:
$$\begin{align*}
1. \quad & \vec{u} + \vec{v} = \vec{v} + \vec{u} && \text{(commutative)} \\
2. \quad & (\vec{u} + \vec{v}) + \vec{w} = \vec{u} + (\vec{v} + \vec{w}) && \text{(associative)} \\
3. \quad & \vec{u} + \vec{0} = \vec{u} && \text{(identity)} \\
4. \quad & \vec{u} + (-\vec{u}) = \vec{0} && \text{(inverse)}
\end{align*}$$

Scalar Multiplication Properties:
$$\begin{align*}
5. \quad & c(\vec{u} + \vec{v}) = c\vec{u} + c\vec{v} && \text{(distributive)} \\
6. \quad & (c + d)\vec{u} = c\vec{u} + d\vec{u} && \text{(distributive)} \\
7. \quad & c(d\vec{u}) = (cd)\vec{u} && \text{(associative)} \\
8. \quad & 1\vec{u} = \vec{u} && \text{(identity)}
\end{align*}$$

Magnitude Properties:
$$\begin{align*}
9. \quad & |c\vec{u}| = |c||\vec{u}| \\
10. \quad & |\vec{u} + \vec{v}| \leq |\vec{u}| + |\vec{v}| && \text{(triangle inequality)}
\end{align*}$$

Problem: Convert the spherical coordinates $(6, \frac{\pi}{4}, \frac{\pi}{3})$ to Cartesian coordinates.

Solution:

Given: $\rho = 6$, $\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}$, $\phi = \frac{\pi}{3}$

Apply conversion formulas:

$$\begin{align*}
x &= \rho\sin\phi\cos\theta = 6\sin\frac{\pi}{3}\cos\frac{\pi}{4} \\
&= 6 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = \frac{6\sqrt{6}}{4} = \frac{3\sqrt{6}}{2}
\end{align*}$$

$$\begin{align*}
y &= \rho\sin\phi\sin\theta = 6\sin\frac{\pi}{3}\sin\frac{\pi}{4} \\
&= 6 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = \frac{3\sqrt{6}}{2}
\end{align*}$$

$$\begin{align*}
z &= \rho\cos\phi = 6\cos\frac{\pi}{3} = 6 \cdot \frac{1}{2} = 3
\end{align*}$$

Answer: \boxed{\left(\frac{3\sqrt{6}}{2}, \frac{3\sqrt{6}}{2}, 3\right)}
